Firstmate: what the brief-intake preflight needs from you
=========================================================

fork/bin/fm-launch-policy's `brief-intake` policy refuses a ship or scout
launch whose brief still carries one of the three intake defects from the
2026-10-29 post-mortem (a slice intent that reads as the whole capability,
an unnamed project check set, and conflicting bounded promises with no
stated composition).
The refusal message names every missing element with the exact line to add,
so a refused launch is fixed by editing the brief and dispatching again.
Nothing outside fork/ changes; the check rides the existing HK1/HK2 seam.

Two things must be in place before a launch meets the check.


1. Enable the policy (once per home)
------------------------------------

config/fork-policies is home config, not tracked code.
Add the line:

  brief-intake

The hooks only run the launch policy when that file exists, and the policy
only judges briefs when the name is enabled there.
With it absent or the name missing, launches behave exactly as before.


2. Supply the markers in every ship and scout brief
---------------------------------------------------

`bin/fm-brief.sh` scaffolds the sections; a correct fill adds these lines
anywhere in the brief (leading indentation or a markdown bullet before the
marker is fine, but the value must sit on the same line):

  Delivers: <what this slice delivers>
      The slice's own behaviour, never the whole capability - the defect
      that made review re-file "it cannot do the thing" every round.
  Defers: <what this slice defers, or nothing>
      The complement, so a partial slice cannot read as the whole ask.
  Checks: <the project's full check set>
      Resolve the project's full check set at intake from its own scripts
      or CI (package.json scripts, the CI workflow), never from memory,
      and list every check the worker must pass before reporting done
      (the bowling slice lost a CI round to a missing format:check).
      A report-only scout may write `Checks: none - report-only`.
  Bound: <one bounded promise - a for-every guarantee or a shared budget>
      One line per bounded promise or rate limit the brief states.
      Fewer than two needs no composition line.
  Composition: <how those bounded promises compose when they conflict, and
                the test that pins that combined promise>
      Required as soon as two or more Bound: lines appear - the defect
      that cost three review rounds finding one starvation route each time.

Secondmate charters are exempt: a charter is a standing contract, not a
slice brief, and the policy never checks one.
A brief that does not exist is also left to upstream's own brief check.

The checker runs standalone too, for a pre-dispatch dry run:

  fork/bin/fm-brief-intake-check data/<task-id>/brief.md

Exit 0 means the brief carries every element it must; exit 1 lists the
missing elements; exit 2 means the file could not be judged.
